addclaim(59,914,4,[52],'19–52','Paraphrase: is flatness typical; it suffices to test finitely generated ideals or universal finite relations.','For an internal module over an internal commutative ring, flatness is expressed geometrically by the finite equational criterion: each relation factors through a finite matrix of relations. It is preserved by geometric inverse image and filtered colimits.','proof sketched; question answered by the displayed criterion','For Σa_i m_i=0 require finitely many n_j and b_ij with m_i=Σb_ij n_j and Σa_i b_ij=0. The disjunction over finite matrix sizes and the existential witnesses are geometric. The usual ideal-injectivity criterion follows by tensoring0→I→A and the reverse implication reduces arbitrary relations to finitely generated ideals.','Stacks00HK proves the exact equational criterion; Stacks058C Theorem10.81.4 gives Lazard’s finite-free filtered-colimit formulation. These are stronger than a mere keyword match to flatness.',['StacksFlatness'])
addclaim(60,914,4,[52],'54–69','« M fid plat est-ce typique ? »','Faithfully flat modules, with all module maps, are not a geometric full subtheory. Faithfully flat unital algebras are a different case.','open on the pages; negative for the full-module interpretation','The filtered sequence Z --2→ Z --2→… has faithfully flat terms but colimit Z[1/2]. Tensoring the colimit with Z/2 gives0, so it is not faithfully flat. Multiplication by2 is a module map but not a unital Z-algebra map; that distinction is essential.','Stacks flatness criteria and the geometric filtered-colimit closure test settle the interpretation by this independent explicit counterexample.',['StacksFlatness','SGA4I1972'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-060')
addclaim(61,914,4,[52,53],'54–69;90–95','Paraphrase: finiteness, freeness, projectivity, invertibility and injectivity as typical properties.','With all module maps, finite generation/presentation, cyclicity, projectivity and invertibility fail geometric filtered-colimit closure. Ordinary categorical injectivity also fails preservation by geometric inverse image; a different internal injectivity convention would need to be specified.','list of questions and negative annotations','The same Z --2→Z sequence has free rank-one stages and non-finitely-generated colimit Z[1/2], which is not projective over Z (a projective abelian group is free, and a nonzero free group cannot be indefinitely2-divisible). For injectivity, pull the injective k-module k from Set to sheaves on a circle. The constant sheaf k embeds in the rank-two local system with monodromy [[1,1],[0,1]], but no invariant splitting exists; hence it is not injective. The matrix obstruction is checked in the certificate.','Stacks0B8I identifies tensor-invertible modules with finite locally free rank1; the explicit colimit and local-system arguments settle these full-property readings. No claim is made that the same examples refute moduli with a chosen basis or duality.',['StacksInvertible','SGA4I1972'],'C')
addclaim(62,914,4,[52],'70–87','Paraphrase: relative algebra properties, and whether a fixed finitely presented algebra can be made flat or smooth by a subtopos.','Integrality, radiciality, surjectivity on spectra, flatness and formal unramifiedness have geometric finite-witness formulations; universal homeomorphism combines integral, radicial and surjective. Smoothness/étaleness without a retained finite-presentation hypothesis must not be conflated with their formal versions.','partly answered list; formal smoothness/étaleness and the quotient construction queried','Integrality has a monic-polynomial witness. For radiciality the diagonal is surjective, so every element of ker(B⊗_A B→B) is nilpotent. Surjectivity is equivalent to rad(IB)∩A=rad(I) for every finitely generated ideal I, expressible by finite equations with an unbounded disjunction of powers. Flatness is C059; Ω_B/A=0 expresses formal unramifiedness. For a fixed finite presentation, flatness can therefore be forced by a geometric quotient; smoothness needs the standard finite-presentation/Jacobian criterion. The unqualified formal-smooth/formal-étale geometric classification is not identified by the source fragment.','Stacks01S4 gives the exact diagonal criterion; Stacks00FF gives spectrum base-change facts; Stacks00UM identifies formal unramifiedness with Ω=0; Stacks00TH Proposition10.138.13 separates formal smoothness from smoothness by finite presentation. The broad remaining formal-property question is retained rather than inferred from ind-smoothness.',['StacksRelativeAlgebra','StacksFlatness'],'C',confidence='medium')
addclaim(63,914,4,[53],'96–117','Paraphrase: a domain need not embed in a field internally; complemented zero should suffice.','In a topos, a domain with decidable zero embeds in its internal fraction field. A subring of a geometric field necessarily has decidable zero. Domain alone does not imply this constructive condition.','necessary condition argued; sufficiency conjectured','If zero is complemented, nonzero elements form a multiplicative subobject; localization at them is a field and the domain map is monic. Conversely a geometric field has complementary zero and units, whose pullback gives decidable zero in a subring. For the generic domain, a nonunit can vanish after quotient to a prime-domain residue, so the universally nonzero elements are only units.','Caramello–Johnstone §1 gives the geometric field decomposition and distinguishes ordinary field models from their internal classifier. The internal fraction argument is reconstructed explicitly with its decidability hypothesis.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009'])
addclaim(64,914,4,[53],'118–132','Paraphrase: invertibility of M⊗Hom(M,A)→A should force finite projectivity and monogenicity.','If the evaluation M⊗Hom_A(M,A)→A is an isomorphism, M is finite locally free of rank1. Monogenicity holds locally, not necessarily globally.','whole proof attempt canceled in the transcription','The preimage of1 is a finite dual-basis sum, so M is finite projective; localization gives rank1. A nonprincipal invertible ideal, for example(2,1+sqrt(-5)) in Z[sqrt(-5)], is locally rank1 but not cyclic: its ideal norm2 would require an element a²+5b²=2, impossible. The finite norm obstruction is recorded. Hom base change is justified after finite projectivity, not assumed before it.','Stacks0B8I proves precisely tensor inverse⇔finite locally free rank1 and identifies the inverse with Hom(M,A). The global-monogenic assertion is refuted by the stated ideal.',['StacksInvertible'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-064')
addclaim(65,915,4,[55,56,60],'137–179;202–217;373–392','Paraphrase: accessible categories and functors are Ind constructions; later the comma-directedness condition is added.','For small C and regular κ, Ind_κ(C) is the free κ-filtered-colimit completion; its κ-presentables are the retract closure of C. An identification of a given E with that particular Ind_κ(C) requires the density and κ-filtered comma presentation. Accessible functors admit such a description at suitable ranks.','initial abbreviated formulation refined by himself on scan60','An object presented by a κ-filtered colimit is κ-presentable only if its identity factors through a stage, hence is a retract. Conversely retracts of representables remain presentable. The canonical comma presentation is κ-filtered in the valid Ind setting. Density alone does not imply Ind of the selected C: {1} is dense in Set but its Ind category is terminal. This does not refute Set’s accessibility with a larger small presentable subcategory.','Lack–Tendas §2, Theorem2.3 and Corollary2.5 give exact Ind/rank statements; Beke Proposition0.1 states the finitely accessible/presheaf-points correspondence. The source’s later stronger formulation is kept as the final version.',['LackTendas2023','BekePresheafType'])
addclaim(66,915,4,[55],'157–170','Paraphrase: an accessible functor preserves presentable objects for every sufficiently large π.','There is a rank α such that an accessible functor preserves β-presentables for every β sharply larger than α. Arbitrary sufficiently large regular cardinals cannot replace this condition.','false as unqualified; no local correction of the quantifier','F(X)=X^N on Set preserves aleph1-filtered colimits. For arbitrarily large singular λ with cf(λ)=omega, a set X of sizeλ is λ^+-presentable but |X^N|=λ^omega>λ by König, so F(X) is not λ^+-presentable. The counterexample uses ordinary regular presentability ranks λ^+.','Lack–Tendas Corollary2.6 p5 is the exact repaired theorem, following Adámek–Rosický Theorem2.19. The sharply-larger restriction is mathematically essential.',['LackTendas2023'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-066')
addclaim(67,915,4,[55,56],'171–184;219–263','Paraphrase: continuous functors are representable or have adjoints; topoi and abelian categories give accessible examples.','On a locally presentable category, a small-colimit-to-limit presheaf is representable; an accessible limit-preserving functor between locally presentable categories is a right adjoint. Accessible finite-limit-preserving Set-valued functors are prorepresentable. Topoi and Grothendieck abelian categories are locally presentable, with all the generator/exactness hypotheses retained.','statements sketched; the generalized generator criterion is repeatedly modified','Accessibility supplies a solution set. The adjoint functor theorem then yields the adjoint, and evaluation at the singleton gives representability for a Set-valued right adjoint. A lex Set functor is a filtered colimit of representables via its cofiltered category of elements, with accessibility supplying smallness. A merely accessible source need not have all limits; a generator plus exact filtered colimits without the other axioms is not the theorem.','Riehl Theorem4.7.10 gives the exact solution-set/SAFT package; Makkai–Pitts and Lack–Power supply locally presentable models. Beke, Sheafifiable Homotopy Model Categories, Proposition3.10 treats Grothendieck abelian categories.',['RiehlContextOnline','LackPower2009','Beke2000'],'C')
addclaim(68,917,4,[60,61],'394–414;427–445','Paraphrase: Fil^{π′}(E) should equal the objects of presentation rank π′.','For κ=aleph1 and C=countable sets, Ind_κ(C)=Set. At singular λ>aleph0 of countable cofinality, some λ^+-presentable sets have no κ-directed presentation by C with a cofinal index set of size≤λ. Thus the unqualified Fil^λ equality fails.','open question at402; later unconditional equality proposed','Let |X|=λ. Such a presentation would yield a cofinal family of≤λ countable subsets of X, since countably many representing stages have a common upper bound. Enumerate this family and choose λ_n↑λ with λ_n<λ. Pick x_n outside the union of the firstλ_n members. The countable set{x_n} is contained in no listed member, contradiction. X is λ^+-presentable in Set.','Lack–Tendas Theorem2.3/Corollary2.5 gives the positive statement at sharply larger ranks. The diagonal counterexample answers the manuscript’s unrestricted question in ZFC; no cardinal-arithmetic assumption is hidden.',['LackTendas2023'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-068')
addclaim(69,917,4,[61,62],'427–460','Paraphrase: bounded filtered subdiagrams and splitting retracts establish the filtration bound.','Ordinary filtered subdiagrams cannot replace κ-directed ones when κ>omega. The cardinal estimate |J|≤(π′′)^{π′} does not imply |J|≤π′′ without an additional bound. The retract argument remains valid for bounded ordinary filtered presentations in a cocomplete finite-colimit-generated setting.','proof sketched; cardinal equality explicitly questioned','Closing a set of stages under κ-small upper bounds may increase its cardinal to a power. C068 shows the increase cannot always be avoided. A retract of an object is an ordinary filtered colimit of its repeated idempotent endomorphism; this countable chain splits the retract but is not generally κ-directed for uncountableκ.','Lack–Tendas2.3–2.6 identifies exactly where sharp cardinal bounds repair the argument. The idempotent-chain construction is an independent elementary proof with its filteredness limit stated.',['LackTendas2023'],'C')
addclaim(70,916,4,[57],'267–324','Paraphrase: dense small subcategories, reflective presheaf embeddings, and a finite-limit condition making C/X filtered.','A small dense C in cocomplete locally small E gives a fully faithful nerve with a left adjoint. But C finitely complete and C→E lex does not make C/X filtered; finite colimits preserved in E give that variance. Essential surjectivity of the colimit extension alone is not density.','valid density equivalences mixed with a wrong limit-direction claim','Take C={1}→Set: it is lex and dense, but C/2 is discrete with two objects, hence not filtered. For the essential-surjectivity warning, C a discrete set of two copies of1 maps to Set; its colimit extension Set²→Set is coproduct and essentially surjective, while the nerve X↦(X,X) is not full. Density itself follows from the canonical counit criterion.','Kelly §5.1/Theorem5.56 gives the exact density theorem. The two finite counterexamples separate the extra assertions and also explain why the earlier 161-1 cofinality shortcut cannot be reused.',['Kelly1982'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-070')
addclaim(71,916,4,[56,59],'255–263;345–359','Paraphrase: Set^op and Ab^op are not accessible, and only the singleton is accessible in Set^op.','The only presentable object of Set^op at any regular rank is a singleton; hence Set^op is not accessible. Ab^op is also not accessible. A strict cogenerator is not automatically a small codense subcategory.','assertions sketched with an inadequate inverse-limit explanation','For nonempty X with at least2 elements, a map2^S→X detecting the all-zero tuple cannot factor through any<κ coordinate projection. For X=empty, use the κ-cofiltered system Inj(J,κ), J⊂κ^+, |J|<κ: all stages nonempty but the limit empty. Thus Hom(-,X) fails the required preservation; singleton Hom is constant1. For Ab^op use the established theorem that an accessible opposite of a locally presentable category forces a small preorder; Ab is not one.','Beke2000 Remark3.3 explicitly invokes Adámek–Rosický1.64 for the opposite-category theorem. The complete Set^op proof, including the empty-object edge case, is supplied here.',['Beke2000','ColimitDense2019'])
addclaim(72,916,4,[59],'345–359','Paraphrase: is every cocomplete category with a small dense subcategory accessible; perhaps Set^op supplies a counterexample?','The statement that every cocomplete bounded category is locally presentable is equivalent to Vopěnka’s principle. Set^op is bounded exactly under the set-theoretic condition(M): some λ has no nonprincipal λ-complete ultrafilter. It is not an unconditional bounded counterexample.','explicit open question; marginal attempted resolution unsupported','Cocomplete accessible means locally presentable, so the exact question is the bounded-cocomplete statement. The 2019 source states its equivalence with VP and separately proves the Set^op criterion. It also corrects the older false assertion that colimit-density alone implies boundedness under VP; these two notions cannot be exchanged.','Rosický–Trnková–Adámek1990 is the backward reference; Adámek et al.2019 Introductionp448 and Corollary3.5p452 explicitly state the equivalence and the correction. Adámek–Rosický1996 gives the related equalizer/connected-limit version under VP.',['ColimitDense2019','AdamekRosicky1996'],'B',event='targeted proof check')
addclaim(73,915,4,[55,56],'180–184;204–217','Paraphrase: accessible categories are stable under 2-limits and perhaps 2-colimits, with pointwise limits of accessible functors.','The 2-category of accessible categories and accessible functors admits flexible limits, pseudolimits and bilimits. Ordinary strict equalizers are a different matter. Pointwise colimits of accessible functors are accessible at a common rank; pointwise limits need a sufficiently suitable rank and target exactness. The blanket bicolimit claim is not settled by the cited limit theorem.','limit assertion stated; bicolimits explicitly queried','Choose one common regular/sharply enlarged rank for the small diagram. Products, inserters, equifiers and the other flexible constructions are accessible; this builds bilimits. Commutation of the relevant small limits with directed colimits at that rank gives the functor-category assertion. Accessibility is not self-dual, so no bicolimit result follows by reversing arrows.','Lack–Tendas Theorem5.5p33 proves the exact limit theorem and cites Makkai–Paré5.1.6; Bird–Kelly–Power–Street1989 is the earlier flexible-limit reference. Searches for arbitrary bicolimits in Acc did not locate an exact unconditional theorem matching the sheet, so that component remains precisely delimited.',['LackTendas2023','BirdKellyPowerStreet1989'],'C')
addclaim(74,916,4,[59],'361–369','Paraphrase: free change of structure and separately continuous bifunctors should be represented by a tensor product.','For locally presentable categories, accessible limit-preserving structure-forgetting functors have left adjoints, and there is a tensor product representing separately cocontinuous bifunctors. A formulation with arbitrary accessible categories and unspecified limits does not follow.','claims stated without local proof','At a common regular rank, use the small presentable subcategories and their bounded cocompletion/tensor universal properties; Ind completion gives the locally presentable tensor. Caviglia–Horel§2 gives the explicit category of separately small-limit-preserving functors and its continuous-functor description. The right-adjoint/continuous formulation is its adjoint dual and needs consistent variance. These hypotheses are stronger than mere accessibility.','Kelly§6.5, formulas(6.24)–(6.28), proves the small bounded-cocomplete tensor construction. Caviglia–Horel§2, pp4–5, gives the exact unenriched locally presentable version. The backward Bird1984 thesis lead was retrieved only as an indexed passage; both full-PDF routes failed503/502, so no uninspected Bird proof is used.',['CavigliaHorel','Kelly1982'],'C')

addproblem(28,'Flat and faithfully flat modules',[occurrence(4,[52],'19–69')],'Are flatness and faithful flatness typical properties?','Flatness is geometric by finite relations; faithful flatness with all module maps is not.','C059–060 and Stacks00HK/058C give the exact positive criterion and Z[1/2] counterexample.',['StacksFlatness','SGA4I1972'])
addproblem(29,'Module finiteness and injectivity conventions',[occurrence(4,[52,53],'54–69;90–95')],'Which finite, free, projective, invertible or injective conditions survive change of topos?','Separate full module-property categories from moduli with chosen structure; ordinary injectivity is not geometrically preserved.','C061 supplies the filtered-colimit and non-split local-system witnesses. The meaning of an alternative internal injectivity predicate is not specified by the fragment.',['StacksInvertible','SGA4I1972'])
addproblem(30,'Relative algebra properties and forcing flatness',[occurrence(4,[52],'70–87')],'Are radicial, surjective, integral, formally smooth/étale and related conditions typical; can a fixed finite presentation be made flat or smooth by a subtopos?','The finite-witness conditions in C062 are geometric; finite presentation must remain fixed in the smooth/étale comparison. The broad formal-property formulation needs a precise internal meaning.','Stacks01S4,00FF,00UM and00TH give exact current criteria; C062 records which translate and which were not identified as one geometric theorem.',['StacksRelativeAlgebra','StacksFlatness'],worth='Worth a focused logical comparison of formal smoothness and formal étaleness; begin with their finite-presentation special case and the exact internal lifting quantifiers.')
addproblem(31,'Internal domains and fractions',[occurrence(4,[53],'96–117')],'Does complemented zero suffice for embedding a domain into a field?','Yes: decidable zero permits localization at all nonzero elements; it is also necessary for a subring of a geometric field.','C063 gives the explicit constructive fraction argument and its generic-domain obstruction.',['CaramelloJohnstone2009'])
addproblem(32,'The canceled tensor-invertible-module argument',[occurrence(4,[53],'118–132','whole block canceled')],'Does invertible evaluation imply finite projectivity and monogenicity?','It implies finite locally free rank1; global monogenicity is false.','Stacks0B8I and C064 give the valid theorem, order of proof and nonprincipal ideal counterexample.',['StacksInvertible'])
addproblem(33,'Rank changes of accessible functors',[occurrence(4,[55,56],'137–170;202–217')],'Do all sufficiently large cardinals work, and which density/Ind formulation is correct?','Use a suitable sharply larger rank and the specified Ind presentation, not every regular rank.','Lack–Tendas2.3–2.6 and C065–066 give the corrected quantifiers and the countable-power counterexample.',['LackTendas2023','BekePresheafType'])
addproblem(34,'Accessible categories under 2-limits and 2-colimits',[occurrence(4,[55],'180–184')],'Do both 2-limits and 2-colimits remain accessible?','Flexible limits and bilimits are established; the arbitrary bicolimit assertion requires an independent theorem.','Lack–Tendas5.5 proves the former. The exact bicolimit searches and their limits are recorded under C073.',['LackTendas2023','BirdKellyPowerStreet1989'],worth='A narrowly formulated bicolimit problem is worthwhile; first fix whether morphisms preserve one common rank or are arbitrary accessible functors.')
addproblem(35,'The generalized generator and representability tests',[occurrence(4,[55,56],'171–179;219–263')],'Which exactness/generator hypotheses make a category locally presentable and force adjoints?','Use locally presentable categories or a full adjoint-functor-theorem solution-set package. The changing generator list on the page is not a complete theorem.','C067 distinguishes prorepresentability, representability and adjoints; Riehl4.7.10 and Beke3.10 provide the exact hypotheses.',['RiehlContextOnline','Beke2000','LackPower2009'])
addproblem(36,'Density and the wrong comma-category variance',[occurrence(4,[57,58],'267–339'),occurrence('1',[19,20],'697–727')],'Does finite-limit closure make C/X filtered, and do the stated colimit relations suffice?','Finite-colimit closure has that variance; use the exact density/comodel criterion for extension.','C020–021,C070 give three separate explicit counterexamples; Kelly5.56 supplies the precise positive theorem.',['Kelly1982'])
addproblem(37,'Cocomplete bounded categories',[occurrence(4,[59],'345–359')],'Is every cocomplete strictly generated category accessible?','With bounded meaning small dense subcategory, the universal statement is equivalent to Vopěnka’s principle.','C072 matches Adámek et al.2019p448 exactly and records the corrected older colimit-density claim; Set^op boundedness is conditional(M).',['ColimitDense2019','AdamekRosicky1996'],worth='The general question is settled at the level of set-theoretic strength; a historical comparison of the manuscript’s exact density terminology is valuable.')
addproblem(38,'The cardinal filtration equality',[occurrence(4,[60,61,62],'394–460')],'Is Fil^{π′}(E)=E_{π′} without a power-closed cardinal bound?','No; the singular countable-cofinality example in Set refutes it. A sharply-larger hypothesis gives the standard positive theorem.','C068–069 proves the diagonal obstruction and identifies the switch from κ-directed to merely filtered diagrams.',['LackTendas2023'])
addproblem(39,'Tensor product and free change of structure',[occurrence(4,[59],'361–369')],'Represent separately continuous bifunctors and give left adjoints to structure change.','The locally presentable version is established, with consistent adjoint variance.','C074 matches the opened Caviglia–Horel§2 and Kelly6.5; arbitrary accessible categories need additional existence hypotheses.',['CavigliaHorel','Kelly1982'])

